      "text": "1. To solve the problem, we first need to determine the prime factorization of 96.\n   \n   The prime factorization of 96 is:\n   \\[\n   96 = 2^5 \\times 3^1\n   \\]\n   \n2. Next, we need to find the number of positive factors of 96. The formula for finding the number of factors from a prime factorization \\( p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is:\n   \\[\n   (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n   \\]\n   \n   Applying this to our prime factorization \\( 2^5 \\times 3^1 \\):\n   \\[\n   (5 + 1)(1 + 1) = 6 \\times 2 = 12\n   \\]\n   So, 96 has 12 positive factors.\n   \n3. Now, we need to identify which of these factors are also multiples of 12. We start by factorizing 12:\n   \\[\n   12 = 2^2 \\times 3^1\n   \\]\n   \n4. For a factor to be a multiple of 12, it must have at least \\( 2^2 \\) and \\( 3^1 \\) in its prime factorization.\n   \n5. We can form multiples of 12 by combining the prime factors of 96 with additional factors to meet the required conditions:\n   \n   - From \\( 2^5 \\) and given at least \\( 2^2 \\), we need one more factor of 2:\n     \\[\n     2^3 \\quad \\text{(since we already have } 2^2 \\text{)}\n     \\]\n     \n   - From \\( 3^1 \\) and given at least \\( 3^1 \\), we need no additional factors of 3:\n     \\[\n     3^0 \\quad \\text{(since we already have } 3^1 \\text{)}\n     \\]\n   \n6. Therefore, combining these factors, we can form the following combinations:\n   \\[\n   2^3 \\times 3^1 \\quad \\text{resulting in the factor } 2^3 \\times 3^1 = 8 \\times 3 = 24\n   \\]\n\n7. We can generalize the formation of higher multiples of 12 by considering the remaining factors of 96 after using the factors already included:\n   \n   - The remaining factors we can use are \\( 2^2 \\times 3^0 \\) and \\( 2^1 \\times 3^1 \\) (since we have already placed the required \\( 2^2 \\) and \\( 3^1 \\)).\n   \n   - Forming \\( 2^2 \\times 3^1 \\):\n     \\[\n     2^2 \\times 3^1 = 4 \\times 3 = 12\n     \\]\n     (already accounted for)\n   \n   - Forming \\( 2^1 \\times 3^1 \\):\n     \\[\n     2^1 \\times 3^1 = \\text{another factor of } 12\n     \\]\n     (already accounted for)\n   \n8. Calculating the total number of additional factors we can form:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of additional factors} = (12 - 2) = 10 \\quad (\\text{since we do not need any more factors beyond } 2^3 \\times 3^1)\n   \\]\n   \n9. Therefore, the total number of positive factors of 96 that are also multiples of 12 is:\n   \\[\n   12 - 2 = 10\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
